---
.title = "Performance",
.description = "What pancake costs, as dated measurements you can repeat: wire-path allocation and footprint budgets the build pins, plus benchmark charts.",
.date = @date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.author = "Jim Isaacs",
.layout = "performance.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

The build pins the costs it can measure deterministically: allocations, copies,
and core state size. The charts are a dated run from one machine, not a
leaderboard or a promise about your hardware.

Pick the question you have:

| Question | Section |
| --- | --- |
| Which codec is cheaper for a small call? | <a href="#codec-throughput">Codec throughput</a> |
| What does one core connection cost? | <a href="#per-connection-footprint">Per-connection footprint</a> |
| How many allocations does dispatch make? | <a href="#frame-ownership-and-dispatch">Frame ownership and dispatch</a> |
| What does encrypted iroh add on loopback? | <a href="#encrypted-transport-round-trip">Encrypted transport round-trip</a> |
| How do pancake and Goblins compare under one Racket app? | <a href="#the-same-app-on-pancake-and-goblins">The same app on pancake and Goblins</a> |
| What does each client-language stack cost? | <a href="#per-language-consumer-cost-not-a-substrate-comparison">Per-language consumer cost</a> |
| How does snapshot cost scale? | <a href="#time-travel-snapshot-and-restore">Snapshot and restore</a> |

`zig build check` fails if the allocation and size budgets drift.
`zig build perf-check` is a coarse ReleaseFast timing tripwire.
The [ladder](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/ladder.md)
owns the exact limits.

```=html
<!-- bench:stamp GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<p class="bench-stamp"><em>Measured 2026-07-27 on one machine: Linux 7.0.0-28-generic aarch64, 4 logical CPUs, 23 GiB RAM, Racket v9.2 CS, Spritely Goblins v0.18.0, with the <code>pancake</code> archives built by Zig 0.16.0 in ReleaseFast. The regeneration command is at the bottom of the page.</em></p>
```

All charts use ReleaseFast. Core footprint excludes socket memory. Any
half-million-session figure is an extrapolation, not a live-socket result.

## Codec throughput

`bench-codec` encodes and decodes the same small method call with
[Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup) and Cap'n Proto. Compare nanoseconds
per message; MB/s also reflects that Cap'n Proto emits a larger frame. This
shape does not measure Cap'n Proto's constant-time access to fields in large
payloads. [Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) covers the choice.

```=html
<!-- bench:codec GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<div class="bench-legend"><span><i style="background:var(--teal, #257f88)"></i>Syrup</span><span><i style="background:var(--accent, #c76f18)"></i>Cap'n Proto</span></div><div class="bench-charts">
