<p class="bench-note">Both runtimes snapshot in O(objects) and stay flat as each actor grows larger (24 µs for 64-byte actors, 26.2 µs for 16384-byte actors). They share each actor's state instead of copying it. These run natively (pancake in Zig, Goblins in Racket), so the absolute times reflect the runtime, not a better algorithm; read the shape, not the race. On restore, pancake frees the rewound copies right away; Goblins leaves that cleanup to its garbage collector.</p>
